WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.320
For the past four decades, Nicolas Cage has
been one of the most prolific and entertaining

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.810
actors in show business.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.720
And this weekend, he’ll bring his singular
style to the exciting action-comedy "The Unbearable

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.510
Weight of Massive Talent," which features
the actor as, well, Nick Cage, an accomplished

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.980
blockbuster action star who's ready to retire.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.051
However, once he's hired to attend the birthday
party of a wealthy superfan named Javi, he

00:00:21.051 --> 00:00:25.300
gets something of a second wind … and is
then recruited by a CIA operative Vivian to

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.467
channel some of his most iconic fictional
characters for a dangerous government mission.

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:33.300
The film marks the culmination of so many
memorable moments from Cage's prodigious screen

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.967
career, from his sprawling adventure stories
to searing action-dramas.

00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:38.313
"Is this supposed to be me?

00:00:38.313 --> 00:00:39.890
It's grotesque.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.360
I'll give you $20,000 for it."

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.470
So to celebrate this marvelously meta role,
here's a look back at Nicolas Cage's best

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.380
action movies of all time, ranked.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.290
Just before the MCU emerged as a mainstay
of the movie industry, Cage became one of

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:58.250
Marvel’s most fiery superheroes of all time
– the title character of 2007's "Ghost Rider."

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.421
Cage stars as Johnny Blaze, a reckless stuntman
who’s forced to become the enforcer of the

00:01:02.421 --> 00:01:04.080
demon Mephistopheles.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.159
"You."

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.280
"Hello, Johnny."

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.359
"Stay away from me."

00:01:11.359 --> 00:01:15.210
Johnny has no choice but to do the devil's
bidding because, as a teenager, he sold his

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:16.670
soul to save his father.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.939
With Johnny Blaze, Cage's performance manages
to make the character both ruthless and redeemable,

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:23.970
haughty and heartfelt, and condemned but not
contemptible.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:27.950
It's a bold balancing act that's perfectly
matched by the visually arresting action scenes.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.330
"Let's ride."

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.689
There’s something so satisfying about seeing
Cage bring his skills to the screen for films

00:01:34.689 --> 00:01:38.830
that have a bit of a supernatural bent, and
"Drive Angry" is another prime example of that.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:44.039
Cage stars in this 2011 3-D film as John Milton,
a man who escapes from Hell armed with nothing

00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:45.749
less than Satan's own gun.

00:01:45.749 --> 00:01:49.709
Milton is on a mission to get revenge against
a cult leader named Jonah King, a devil worshiper

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:53.429
who’s murdered Milton's daughter and stolen
his baby granddaughter in order to sacrifice

00:01:53.429 --> 00:01:55.369
the child and bring about Hell on earth.

00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:59.170
It's clear throughout the flick that Cage
has a lot of fun portraying this unholy, semi-human

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.459
character who has absolutely nothing left
to lose — not even his own soul, which makes

00:02:03.459 --> 00:02:08.039
for some hellishly good action and stylish
fight sequences with an otherworldly flair.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.979
Better buckle up for this high-speed film
ride.

00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:23.390
When it comes to Cage's sci-fi surprises,
2009's "Knowing" is a must-see.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:27.459
In the film, he portrays John Koestler, a
grieving widower and celebrated astrophysics

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:30.950
professor who gets his hands on a bizarre
list of dates and coordinates that relate

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.945
to major catastrophes throughout recent history.

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:41.348
"9/11/01?"

00:02:41.348 --> 00:02:45.220
But he soon discovers that the numbers don't
only relate to past calamities – they predict

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.220
future ones.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:49.629
He finds himself right in the thick of the
chaos, and begins to suspect that the numbers

00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:52.939
point to some dark moments for mankind in
the near future.

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:56.210
As John scrambles to protect his child from
the voices that are creeping into his own

00:02:56.210 --> 00:03:00.670
head and the impending disaster, Cage delivers
a searing performance as a parent who has

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.840
to grapple with the impossible before it's
too late.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.110
"How am I supposed to stop the end of the
world?"

00:03:05.648 --> 00:03:10.329
John Woo's 2002 war film "Windtalkers" centers
on the Navajo code talkers who helped lead

00:03:10.329 --> 00:03:14.640
the troops to victory in World War II's Pacific
theater by using a code that was indecipherable

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:15.900
to enemy forces.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.450
In the film, Cage delivers a riveting performance
as Sgt.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:22.500
Joe Enders, who’s assigned to the crucial
undercover mission of protecting a code talker,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.830
Private Ben Yahzee, from being captured at
all costs.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:28.940
When he and his fellow Marines find themselves
in an ambush, Enders has to decide whether

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:32.390
to follow direct orders or forge his own path
forward through the skirmish.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.690
As Enders, Cage brings both valor and vulnerability
to the role, and what results is a poignant

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.774
picture about a critical part of the Allied
success in the war.

00:03:40.357 --> 00:03:44.409
Some of Cage's best roles are those that allow
him to show who he is, rather than spelling

00:03:44.409 --> 00:03:46.810
out the nuances of the character through dialogue.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:51.360
In 2013's "Joe," he plays something of an
antihero with a mysterious, checkered past.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.909
Although Joe prefers to keep his own company,
he takes a shine to a young man who stumbles

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:56.939
onto his worksite looking for a job.

00:03:56.939 --> 00:04:00.739
The boy, Gary, is hard-working but troubled,
and soon, Joe finds out why.

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:02.660
The kid’s father is an abusive alcoholic.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:06.660
Though Joe is initially inclined to stay out
of it, as the details of Gary's home life

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:10.599
come into view, he has no choice but to act,
putting his own peace and security at risk.

00:04:10.599 --> 00:04:15.859
"And if I find out something's happening to
that boy, I'm gonna whip whoever's ass had

00:04:15.859 --> 00:04:16.880
something to do with it."

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.840
Put simply, Joe isn’t your average Joe,
nor your prototypical heroic figure, and Cage's

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.600
understated performance perfectly captures
the character in this thoughtful action drama.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:31.140
In the 1993 neo-noir thriller “Red Rock
West,” Cage plays Michael Williams, a combat

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.090
veteran who stumbles into the wrong place
at the wrong time.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.900
When he heads to the town of Red Rock, Wyoming,
in search of work, he gets way more than he

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.020
bargained for when he’s mistaken for a hitman.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.498
But when the actual hitman – portrayed by
Dennis Hopper – arrives, the real chaos begins.

00:04:44.498 --> 00:04:47.990
Soon, Michael finds himself in the middle
of a deadly domestic dispute, with a hitman

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.770
and a powerful, rage-filled sheriff on his
tail.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.650
This stylish little thriller has a bit of
everything – high-speed chases, clever jailbreaks,

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:59.060
and some very surprising twists, and Cage
is as charismatic as ever in the role.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.720
If you want to see a film that allows Cage
to channel every inch of his ability to become

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.860
unhinged on-screen, look no further than Werner
Herzog's jarring crime drama "Bad Lieutenant:

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.120
Port of Call New Orleans."

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.660
"Shoot him again."

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:12.689
"What for?"

00:05:12.689 --> 00:05:17.389
The film features the actor as one of the
Big Easy's most corrupt cops, Terence McDonagh.

00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:20.800
Terence might’ve started out his career
as a by-the-book police officer, but throughout

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.930
the shocking events of the film, we watch
him unwind into a spectacular disaster whose

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.310
errant energy sends shockwaves throughout
the city.

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:30.710
He also deals with his own deteriorating grasp
on reality.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:35.550
As the consequences of his bad behaviors bubble
up and his grip on sanity continues to wane,

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.820
Terence chooses to lean into his warped views
of justice, leading to one breathlessly messy

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:43.462
action-adventure and a truly riveting screen
turn for Cage fans.

00:05:44.001 --> 00:05:48.250
The "National Treasure" series is electric
from start to finish, and is a definite must-watch

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:49.669
for Nicolas Cage fans.

00:05:49.669 --> 00:05:54.039
In the first film, released in 2004, Cage's
character, Benjamin Franklin Gates, decides

00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:57.860
he must steal the Declaration of Independence
in order to protect it – and the treasure

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.260
map on the back of it.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:03.050
Meanwhile, in the second pic from 2007, he
has to kidnap the actual president of the

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.362
United States in order to get his hands on
another map that leads to a fabled city of gold.

00:06:07.362 --> 00:06:11.440
In both films, Cage imbues the character with
a true sense of honor, unstoppable charm,

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.290
and a commitment to protecting the most precious
pieces of the past — even if that means

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.690
being completely reckless along the way.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:23.259
"Here's to the men who did what was considered
wrong, in order to do what they knew was right."

00:06:23.259 --> 00:06:27.979
With centuries-old conspiracies and some larger-than-life
set pieces, the "National Treasure" series

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:30.300
is a wholesome double adventure for all ages.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:35.770
The star-studded 2000 smash hit film "Gone
in 60 Seconds" has a whole lot of glorious

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:36.860
roadway action.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:41.069
Cage stars in the film as Randall "Memphis"
Raines, a retired car thief who has to quickly

00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:45.229
assemble a crew who can help him track down
and steal 50 specific luxury cars in the space

00:06:45.229 --> 00:06:47.539
of just three days to save his brother's life.

00:06:47.539 --> 00:06:51.490
Together, the group puts pedal to the metal
to organize a speedy series of heists to take

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.729
place simultaneously across the city of Long
Beach, California.

00:06:54.729 --> 00:06:58.770
What results is a smashing series of highway
chases involving some of the snazziest cars

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.240
that have ever been seen on screen.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.699
And Cage is, as always, at his best as a smooth-talking
criminal who knows exactly what he wants.

00:07:05.699 --> 00:07:10.082
"I don't have one here, however I do have
one in the warehouse."

00:07:10.082 --> 00:07:14.060
"Superb. What else do you have in the warehouse?"

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:19.032
Superhero movies may come in all shapes and
sizes, but 2010's "Kick-Ass" is still one of a kind.

00:07:19.032 --> 00:07:22.139
"I didn't say anything to anyone, I swear."

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:23.879
"Good move, Ass Kick."

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:28.110
The film centers on a nerdy kid named Dave,
who decides to try a superheroic alter ego

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:30.360
on for size — because why not?

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.860
The thing Dave doesn't know, though, is that
he isn't the only person training to deliver

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:35.460
some homegrown justice.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.150
Amid his roughshod crime-fighting adventures,
Kick-Ass eventually meets Hit-Girl and her

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:42.110
vengeance-seeking father, Big Daddy, played
by Cage.

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.990
As Big Daddy, Cage is a real riot, parading
around in a Batman-esque suit and coaching

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:50.150
his daughter on how to destroy armies of goons
all by herself.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:54.479
The role is darkly comedic and shows off Cage's
sublime gift for blending action sequences

00:07:54.479 --> 00:07:57.800
with quirky anecdotes and some very off-the-wall
character moments.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.759
"But it's really no more painful than a punch
in the chest."

00:08:00.759 --> 00:08:02.030
"I hate getting punched in the chest."

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:03.319
"You're gonna be fine, baby doll."

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:09.570
Cage is particularly gifted at playing characters
who have a lack of scruples and absolutely

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:14.853
no regrets, and that's certainly the defining
characteristic of his character in 2005's "Lord of War."

00:08:14.853 --> 00:08:19.889
Cage stars in the pic as Yuri Orlov, a successful
gunrunner who delights in providing arms to

00:08:19.889 --> 00:08:21.650
some of the world's most dangerous individuals.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:27.622
"By the mid '80s, my weapons were represented
in eight of the world's top ten warzones."

00:08:28.084 --> 00:08:32.479
Alongside his brother, Yuri becomes a major
player in the 1982 Lebanon War and catches

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:36.120
the attention of Interpol, and, well, let's
just say he finds himself in the middle of

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.029
some very dangerous conflicts.

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:42.279
As Yuri, Cage is a complete enigma, bringing
both humor and horror to a story that's truly

00:08:42.279 --> 00:08:43.669
engaging and intelligent.

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:45.846
"Can you bring me the gun of Rambo?"

00:08:45.846 --> 00:08:47.368
"Part one, two, or three?"

00:08:47.744 --> 00:08:52.958
Ridley Scott’s 2003 black comedy “Matchstick
Men” features Cage at the absolute top of his game.

00:08:52.958 --> 00:08:58.140
His character, Roy Waller, is at once a cavalier
con artist and a desperately unraveling individual

00:08:58.140 --> 00:08:59.519
with a lonely home life.

00:08:59.519 --> 00:09:03.010
His ability to smooth-talk unsuspecting marks
into handing over their hard-earned money

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:07.230
is impressively strong, but he’s also hanging
on by a thread when it comes to his own sanity

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:08.230
and sense of purpose.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:10.940
"I know, but this is an emergency."

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:11.940
"Hey buddy, ever heard of a line?"

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:16.102
"Hey, have you ever been dragged down the
sidewalk and beaten until you pissed blood?"

00:09:16.102 --> 00:09:19.630
Things take a major turn for Roy when he’s
introduced to the teen daughter he didn't

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:23.820
know he had, Angela, and decides to embrace
his dad status and teach her all he knows

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:24.972
about running a game.

00:09:24.972 --> 00:09:26.642
"You're ready for this."

00:09:26.642 --> 00:09:27.752
"I was born ready."

00:09:27.752 --> 00:09:29.676
"Whew, glad I missed that day."

00:09:29.832 --> 00:09:33.290
But as the stakes of his heists reach new
heights and his mental health continues to

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:36.779
waver, Roy has to worry about more than just
his own financial interests for the first

00:09:36.779 --> 00:09:37.839
time in his life.

00:09:37.839 --> 00:09:42.180
Things eventually culminate into a gloriously
chaotic climax with some dynamite twists that

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:43.761
keep you guessing right until the end.

00:09:44.527 --> 00:09:49.370
1987's "Raising Arizona" was one of Nicolas
Cage's first major films, but it contains

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:52.060
one of his all-time greatest comedic-action
performances.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:56.050
The movie, which helped bring the Coen brothers
into the mainstream, features the star as

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.680
"Hi" McDunnough, a former convict who marries
a police officer named Ed.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:03.850
After they decide they want to have children
but are unable to bear or adopt any themselves,

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:08.000
Hi and Ed decide to kidnap one of the quintuplet
sons of a famous furniture magnate and raise

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.000
him as their own.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.060
Though they initially try to raise him as
little "Junior" and pass him off as their

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:16.230
own biological child, things get completely
out of hand once the couple is reacquainted

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:20.600
with some of Hi's former pals from prison,
as well as other wildcard characters like

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:23.500
Hi's offensive foreman and a monstrous biker
from Hell.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:27.250
From there, poor Junior becomes the unwitting
centerpiece of some blackly hilarious and

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:28.550
action-packed exchanges.

00:10:28.882 --> 00:10:31.337
"Son, you got a panty on your head."

00:10:31.337 --> 00:10:33.695
"Just drive fast, okay?"

00:10:36.768 --> 00:10:40.899
It's not often that a movie with intense fighting
and a complicated game of cat and mouse can

00:10:40.899 --> 00:10:45.460
also be a meaningful meditation on grief,
but that's exactly what "Pig" manages to pull off.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:50.320
The film features Cage as Rob, a man who lives
completely off the grid in the forest alongside

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:51.320
his prized pig.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:55.240
They spend their days hunting truffles, but
when his talented pig is stolen amid a violent

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:59.340
attack, he has to return to the city to face
his mysterious past in order to save her.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.860
"I'm looking for a truffle pig."

00:11:03.706 --> 00:11:06.940
"I don't, I don't understand."

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:10.750
Though the film is dripping with dread and
features some very satisfying moments of action,

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:13.930
it’s also refreshingly subversive and emotionally
engaging.

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:17.710
Anchoring it all is Cage's sensational and
critically revered leading performance in

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:22.139
the pic, which proves that even as accomplished
as he already is, he's still got many surprises

00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:23.360
up his sleeve.

00:11:23.898 --> 00:11:28.490
Nicolas Cage has always had some strong rock-and-roll
energy, and the 2018 horror flick “Mandy”

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:29.950
is a prime example of that.

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:33.970
The psychedelic film features the actor as
Red Miller, a reclusive logger who’s content

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:36.200
to spend his quiet life with his girlfriend,
Mandy.

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.800
Their peace is completely shattered, however,
when a terrifying hippie cult attacks the

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:42.000
couple in their home with the help of some
demonic bikers.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:47.110
In addition to being a gonzo revenge story,
the film is a gory, scary sensory experience,

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:50.737
with Cage's deliciously berserk performance
bringing all the batty action home.

00:11:53.493 --> 00:11:54.955
"Welcome to the Rock."

00:11:54.955 --> 00:12:00.000
The cast of 1996's "The Rock" is a veritable
who's who of action movie all-stars from the era.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.800
Cage stars as FBI Special Agent Dr. Stanley
Goodspeed, a lab geek who has to work alongside

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:08.939
a group of Navy SEALs and a highly classified
spy turned prisoner — played by Sean Connery

00:12:08.939 --> 00:12:13.000
— to infiltrate the notorious Alcatraz prison
after it's taken over by a group of dangerous

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.070
former Marines.

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:18.389
Audiences are treated to a taut thrillride
packed with plenty of punches and, as is the

00:12:18.389 --> 00:12:21.389
custom in any good Cage movie, a lot of iconic
moments.

00:12:21.927 --> 00:12:25.960
For a film that manages to incorporate all
of the trappings of a traditional action film,

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.800
John Woo's "Face/Off" is still an incredibly
original thrill.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:32.750
Sure, you may have seen planes crash into
hangars, high-speed boat chases, and mega

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:36.200
shootouts on-screen before, but chances are
good that you’ve never seen these events

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:39.760
unfold during a blood feud between two people
wearing each other's faces.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:43.158
At least, not until you check out this 1997
classic.

00:12:43.158 --> 00:12:46.660
"You know, I can, uh, eat a peach for hours."

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:50.779
The film features Cage and John Travolta playing
dual roles, with Cage starting off as the

00:12:50.779 --> 00:12:54.949
ruthless criminal Castor Troy before his face
is surgically switched with John Travolta's

00:12:54.949 --> 00:12:56.300
FBI Agent Sean Archer.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:59.780
With their roles reversed and each man pretending
to be the other around their family members

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:04.810
and colleagues, the performances of Cage and
Travolta are exquisitely nuanced and off-kilter.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:08.320
"Wow, we've got something in common."

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:11.870
They also both seem to be having the absolute
time of their lives in these roles, which

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:15.800
amplifies the rush factor as this memorable
action ride brings the duo together for an

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:17.540
epic – that's right – face-off.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:22.649
There are many, many roles that seem to have
been specifically crafted for Nicolas Cage's

00:13:22.649 --> 00:13:27.320
unique screen talents, but 1997's "Con Air"
truly hinges on his particular gifts.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.860
Here, he stars as Cameron Poe, a decorated
combat veteran who’s sent to prison after

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.300
drunkenly killing a man who attacked his wife.

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:37.649
While incarcerated, he ends up on board a
prison transport plane with the country's

00:13:37.649 --> 00:13:41.430
most hardened criminals, who scheme to take
over the plane and escape from custody.

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:44.839
For Cage fans, this film has a little bit
of everything that makes him such a Hollywood

00:13:44.839 --> 00:13:45.839
legend.

00:13:45.839 --> 00:13:49.930
The character is tough but vulnerable, serious
but also self-aware, and he has some unforgettable

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:51.730
one-liners that only he can deliver.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:57.243
"I said, put the bunny back in the box."

00:13:57.243 --> 00:14:01.002
Put simply, when it comes to Nic Cage's most
iconic action films, "Con Air" truly nails

00:14:01.002 --> 00:14:02.002
the landing.

00:14:02.002 --> 00:14:06.340
With all of these amazing performances under
his belt, it's no wonder Cage manages to corral

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:10.230
such intense fandemonium from the likes of
Javi in "The Unbearable Weight of Massive

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:14.579
Talent," a must-see theatrical experience
you should catch with friends in all its action-packed

00:14:14.579 --> 00:14:16.580
glory this weekend.

